<Page title="About this atlas" path="about/"
  description="How the Normandy 1944 atlas works, how the map and the areas of control were drawn, where the figures come from, and what to bear in mind.">
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>About this atlas</h1>
    <p class="lede">An animated map of the Battle of Normandy, from the eve of D-Day on 5 June 1944 to the crossing of the Seine at the end of August: eighty-five days in eighteen moments.</p>

    <h2>Using it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Play</strong> runs through the campaign, pausing on each moment long enough to read it. Press again to pause.</li>
      <li>The <strong>arrows</strong> on screen, or the ← and → keys, step one moment at a time. Space plays and pauses; Home and End jump to the start and finish.</li>
      <li>Click any point on the <strong>timeline</strong> to jump there.</li>
      <li><strong>Drag</strong> to pan, and scroll or pinch to zoom. The next moment resets the view.</li>
      <li>Click a <strong>marker</strong> on the map for a short note about it.</li>
      <li>Every moment has its own address, such as <a href={withBase('cobra/')}>/cobra/</a>, so you can link straight to it. The browser's Back button steps back through the moments you visited.</li>
      <li>Prefer to read? <a href={withBase('story/')}>The whole story</a> is also on a single page, without the map.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How the map is drawn</h2>
    <p>The land, rivers and borders come from Natural Earth; the French coastline is the more detailed outline from IGN and INSEE open data. A few small Normandy rivers that matter to the story, such as the Orne and the Dives, were traced by hand.</p>
    <p>The coloured areas show Allied control. There are five of them, one for each landing force, and they grow into the armies those forces became:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Utah: US VII Corps, later the US Third Army's drive into Brittany and the long hook east</li>
      <li>Omaha: US V Corps, then the US First Army</li>
      <li>Gold: British XXX Corps, then the British Second Army</li>
      <li>Juno: 3rd Canadian Division, then the First Canadian Army</li>
      <li>Sword: British I Corps and the 6th Airborne Division east of the Orne</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Each area is redrawn at a dozen points in the campaign and morphs smoothly between them. The dark line around them is the front. Both are simplified from period situation maps: they show the shape of the campaign, not the exact line on a given day, and they leave out pockets, salients and the no-man's-land between the armies. Arrows show the direction of the main moves, not particular routes.</p>

    <p>Three battles, Epsom, Goodwood and Cobra, are told as close-ups in three steps each, and the hedgerow fighting has its own map. These show formations as simplified military symbols (a cross for infantry, an oval for armour), with start lines, ridges, roads and bombing zones. The positions of formations are approximate, placed from the accounts listed on the Sources page rather than from period maps, and the village names are there to help you find your way.</p>

    <h2>The argument over Caen</h2>
    <p>This atlas tells the campaign the way Montgomery described his plan. The British and Canadians would hold a hinge at Caen and draw in the German armour, so that the Americans could break out in the west. The evidence for that reading is strong:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The plan came first.</strong> Before the invasion, Montgomery briefed a campaign that would pivot on Caen, with the British and Canadians forming a shoulder to attract and defeat German counter-attacks.</li>
      <li><strong>He said so at the time.</strong> On 30 June he wrote that his policy had always been “to draw the main enemy forces in to the battle on our eastern flank, and to fight them there, so that our affairs on the western flank could proceed the easier”.</li>
      <li><strong>The Germans did what he wanted.</strong> On the eve of Cobra, seven German armoured divisions faced the British and Canadians and two faced the Americans: about 750 tanks against 190.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The price was paid above all by British and Canadian infantry, whose casualty rates at times rivalled Passchendaele. What it bought was an American front that the panzers could not reinforce.</p>
    <p>Not everyone accepts this account. Critics, most influentially Carlo D’Este in <cite>Decision in Normandy</cite> (1983), argue that Montgomery expected to take Caen and break out much sooner, and that the “master plan” was partly tidied up afterwards. They point to Caen, a D-Day objective that took more than a month to capture, and to Goodwood, when Montgomery’s messages to Eisenhower promised more than his written orders did. Those points are fair. But whatever Montgomery hoped for on a given day, the German armour went to Caen and stayed there, and that is what made Cobra possible.</p>
    <p>The gauge under the day counter shows the balance of German armour between the two fronts wherever a source records it. The <a href={withBase('sources/')}>Sources</a> page gives the evidence behind each count.</p>

    <h2>Where the figures come from</h2>
    <p>The panel for each moment gives troop numbers, casualties and other figures. Each one links to the <a href={withBase('sources/')}>Sources</a> page, which says where it comes from and whether it has been checked. A few are marked <em>unverified</em>: they are plausible and widely repeated, but have not yet been traced to a source. Sources often differ, especially on German strength and losses; where they do, the range or the difference is noted.</p>
    <p>The running total of Allied troops ashore under the date is interpolated between five sourced figures (D-Day, 11 June, 30 June, 25 July and 21 August), so on the days between them it is an estimate.</p>

    <h2>Accessibility</h2>
    <p>If your device is set to reduce motion, the map moves straight to each moment without animation. Everything can be operated from the keyboard, and the <a href={withBase('story/')}>story page</a> carries all the text and figures in a simple, readable form.</p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>Spotted a mistake, or can trace one of the unverified figures? Please <a href="https://github.com/mattyboomboom/Normandy44/issues">open an issue on GitHub</a>.</p>

    <h2>Credits</h2>
    <p class="small">Map data: <a href="https://www.naturalearthdata.com/">Natural Earth</a> (public domain) and <a href="https://github.com/gregoiredavid/france-geojson">france-geojson</a> (from IGN and INSEE open data). Rendering: <a href="https://d3js.org/">d3</a>. Built with <a href="https://astro.build/">Astro</a>. Type: Big Shoulders Stencil Display and Source Serif 4, both under the SIL Open Font License.</p>
  </div>
</Page>
